8714 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have that\n\n$$\nP(x)⇔Q(x)≡(P(x)∧Q(x))∨(¬P(x)∧¬Q(x)),\n$$\n\n \n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note the following:\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & \\le 7 \\\\ 2x & \\le 6 \\\\ x & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is\n${T}_{P}=(-\\infty ,3]$\nand its complement is\n${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=(3,\\infty ).$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n${T}_{Q}=(1,\\infty )$ \nand its complement is\n${\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}=(-\\infty ,1].$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} & =(-\\infty ,3]\\cap (1,\\infty ) \\\\  & =(1,3] \\\\ {\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q} & =(3,\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,1] \\\\  & =∅ \\\\ {T}_{P⇔Q} & =({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}) \\\\  & =(1,3]\\cup ∅ \\\\  & =(1,3].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199051 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199051 #edb/ref 1061 144906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199051 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49a55e7b-da8f-5b9b-aca0-ea5672a75f90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199051 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199052 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199052 #edb/ref 1061 144907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199052 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5bf2cacb-1ce2-5c76-9cc6-0e6f2a29dc49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199052 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199053 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199053 #edb/ref 1061 144910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199053 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62147a48-4a50-55e4-a548-b950bc9af5a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199053 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199054 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199054 #edb/ref 1061 144911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199054 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f2be205-1493-56f5-8512-a42f382c50fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199054 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199055 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199055 #edb/ref 1061 144912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199055 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e2b4e61-5f88-5898-bcf9-9d4fae86c41e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199055 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199056 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199056 #edb/ref 1061 144915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199056 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b35ba584-56c8-537f-b6d7-15de6671592a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199056 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199057 #edb/ref 1061 144916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199057 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc5d356c-d8d2-517d-839c-18b823820728" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199057 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199525 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **implication** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows: $$P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Rightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$$\n\nThis implication is false whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is false for a specific set of respective variable assignments $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nNow, recall the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$P\\Rightarrow Q \\equiv \\lnot P \\lor Q$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P\\Rightarrow Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\ & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\ & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following universal set:\n\n$$U = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9 \\big\\}$$\n\nLet's define the following predicates over $U{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is prime\n- $Q(x):$ $9$ is divisible by $x$\n\nTo find the truth set of $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x),$ we note the following:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = \\left\\{ 2,3,5,7 \\right\\}.$ As a result, the complement is $$\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{2,3,5,7}}={1,4,6,8,9}.\\end{aligned}$$\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is $$T_Q = \\big\\{ 1,3,9 \\big\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P\\Rightarrow Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\ & ={1,4,6,8,9}\\cup {1,3,9} \\\\ & ={1,3,4,6,8,9}.\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199525 #edb/ref 1065 7992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199525 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "06a295ff-82fc-5afe-8aa1-b24421f2c474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199525 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199526 #edb/ref 1064 "Since implication and equivalence of predicates are simply generalizations of logical operations on statements, all the properties of these operations can be translated to the corresponding operations on predicates.\n\nIn particular, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}P\\Rightarrow Q & ≡¬P∨Q \\\\ P⇔Q & ≡(P\\Rightarrow Q)∧(Q\\Rightarrow P) \\\\ & ≡(¬P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨P) \\\\ & ≡(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧Q)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, let's remind ourselves of the grammatical constructions for implications and biconditionals that we've encountered so far:\n\nFor conditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Rightarrow Q$\n- *If $P,$ then $Q$*\n- *$P$ implies $Q$*\n\nFor biconditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$\n- *$P$ is equivalent to $Q$*\n- *$P$ if and only if $Q$*\n- *If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199526 #edb/ref 1065 7993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199526 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "599dc11e-cbd2-596c-bb72-4f73b83034ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199526 #edb/ref 1067 "Logical Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences With Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199527 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Again, we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **equivalence** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows: $$P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$$\n\nThis predicate is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values for a specific set of variable assignments $x_1,x_2,\\ldots, x_n.$\n\n$$P \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)$$\n\nLet's look at some examples. Assume $x\\in\\mathbb R$ throughout.\n\n- The following statement is an equivalence (i.e., it is true). $$x + 2 = 5 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=3$$ This statement is true because $$\\left(x+2=5\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=3\\right)\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\left(x=3\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x+2 = 5\\right).$$\n- The following statement is **not** an equivalence (i.e., it is false). $$x^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=2$$ This statement is false because $$\\left(x=2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x^2 = 4\\right) \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\left(x^2=4\\quad \\not\\Rightarrow\\quad x=2\\right).$$ However, the following statement is true: $$x^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=\\pm 2$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199527 #edb/ref 1065 7995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199527 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5e9f21f0-8dbb-5442-968e-b1fb4425fef7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199527 #edb/ref 1067 "Equivalence of Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199603 #edb/ref 1064 "For predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ the equivalence $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value.\n\n$$P \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)$$\n\nIt follows that the truth set of $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ can be found using\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}).\\end{aligned}$$\n\nLet's find the truth set of $P(x) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $1-5x < 11$\n- $Q(x):$ $x \\geq -1$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the corresponding inequality: $$\\begin{aligned}1-5x & <11 \\\\ -5x & <10 \\\\ x & >-2\\end{aligned}$$ So, the truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = (-2, \\infty)$ and its complement is $\\overline{T}_P = (-\\infty,-2].$\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is $T_Q = [-1, \\infty)$ and its complement is $\\overline{T}_Q = (-\\infty,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} & =(-2,\\infty )\\cap [-1,\\infty ) \\\\ & =[-1,\\infty ) \\\\ {\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q} & =(-\\infty ,-2]\\cap (-\\infty ,-1) \\\\ & =(-\\infty ,-2] \\\\ {T}_{P⇔Q} & =({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}) \\\\ & =[-1,\\infty )\\cup (-\\infty ,-2] \\\\ & =(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [-1,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199603 #edb/ref 1065 9146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199603 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "196ed409-81bb-5e86-bf1c-5e34aa4fb041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199603 #edb/ref 1067 "Truth Sets of Equivalences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407572 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407572 #edb/ref 1043