15185 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415185 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415185 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415185 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415185 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415185 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9bd67cf2-d2ad-524e-abec-5dd4e6b10fcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415186 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415186 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415186 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415186 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415186 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415186 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415186 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415186 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415186 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c0c1a066-5f9e-50ca-b88c-ecae2c88919a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415187 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415187 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415187 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415187 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $x\\ge 2.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $-x\\le -2.$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ and if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$\n- $x\\ge 2$ is equivalent to $-x\\le -2.$\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ or if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415187 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d6a13d3-ad69-5578-b6a3-a3b3842749f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415187 #edb/ref 1074 "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\ge 2$ if and only if $-x\\le -2.$\n\n- $x\\ge 2$ is equivalent to $-x\\le -2.$\n\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ and if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415188 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415188 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183351" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415188 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415188 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If $x$ is a multiple of $10,$ then $x$ is a multiple of $5.$\n\nWhich of the following is logically equivalent to the conditional predicate above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415188 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54b12d24-977a-54a5-aff6-9e8057c5e326" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415188 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is a multiple of $10.$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is a multiple of $5.$\n\nThen, our initial sentence can be written as $P(x)⇒Q(x),$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬P(x)∨Q(x).\n$$\n\nThe negation of $P(x)$ can be written as\n\n- $¬P(x):$ $x$ is not is a multiple of $10.$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is not a multiple of $10,$ or $x$ is a multiple of $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415189 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415189 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415189 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of $P(x)⇒Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the universal set $R:$ \n\n- $P(x):$ ${x}^{2}\\le 9$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $5x-3>7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f02a6ca7-3a4a-592a-bdf2-d7236cdc3430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415189 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $P(x)⇒Q(x)≡¬P(x)∨Q(x),$ the corresponding truth sets can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note the following:\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\le 9 \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is \n${T}_{P}=[-3,3].$ \nAs a result, the complement is\n${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- To find the the truth set of $Q(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-3 & >7 \\\\ 5x & >10 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $Q(x)$ is\n${T}_{Q}=(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =((-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ))\\cup (2,\\infty ) \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (2,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415190 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415190 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "079b11ff-7cb7-562a-8580-d39e3f97c28c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415190 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415191 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65fffe08-428d-5e94-964d-b84678836fdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415191 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415187 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
